{
  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "q1",
      "text": "What does `return` do in a function?",
      "options": [
        "It gives a value back to the place where the function was called",
        "It prints the value on the screen",
        "It runs the function again from the top",
        "It saves the value in a variable called return"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q2",
      "text": "What does this program print?",
      "code": "def add(a, b):\n    return a + b\n\nadd(2, 3)",
      "options": [
        "5",
        "Nothing: the value comes back, but nothing prints it",
        "add(2, 3)",
        "It stops with an error"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q3",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "def add(a, b):\n    return a + b\n\nresult = add(2, 3) * 10\nprint(result)",
      "options": ["5", "15", "50", "add(2, 3) * 10"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q4",
      "text": "What is the difference between a function that prints its result and one that returns it?",
      "options": [
        "None: they do the same thing",
        "A returned value can be kept and used further, a printed one only shows on the screen",
        "A printed value can go into a calculation, a returned one can't",
        "Only a function that prints can take parameters"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q5",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "def f():\n    return 1\n    print(\"never printed\")\n\nprint(f())",
      "options": ["1\nnever printed", "never printed\n1", "never printed", "1"],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q6",
      "text": "What happens to the lines written after `return` in the same block?",
      "options": [
        "They run after the value is given back",
        "They run only the second time the function is called",
        "Python stops with an error",
        "They never run: the function ends as soon as it reaches return"
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q7",
      "text": "What does a function without `return` give back?",
      "options": [
        "0",
        "An empty text",
        "None, Python's way of saying \"nothing\"",
        "The last value it printed"
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q8",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "def f():\n    print(\"hello\")\n\nresult = f()\nprint(result)",
      "options": ["hello\nNone", "hello\nhello", "None", "hello"],
      "answer": 0
    },
